::CW_U05_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Publication context: A student finishes typing a rough draft and wants to place it in the class online publication space immediately. What should happen first?{
~A. Post it immediately because typing means it is finished.#Incorrect. A typed draft still needs revision, format, and readiness checks.
~B. Delete the draft and start over.#Incorrect. The draft may be useful; it needs a publication workflow.
=C. Review purpose, feedback, format, audience appropriateness, and final conventions before publication.#Correct. Publication-ready work requires more than simply posting a draft.
~D. Change the title only and ignore the rest.#Incorrect. A title is important, but it is not the only publication check.
}

::CW_U05_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Collaborative publication context: Which role checks whether line breaks, paragraph breaks, title, and spacing fit the creative form?{
~A. Weather reporter.#Incorrect. This is not a publication role. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Format checker.#Correct. The format checker reviews whether the piece's visual and structural choices support the form.
~C. Unrelated reader.#Incorrect. The role needs a specific publication responsibility.
~D. Person who changes the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. Collaboration should not erase the writer's purpose.
}

::CW_U05_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A peer suggests adding a loud dramatic ending to a poem meant to feel quiet and reflective. What should the writer do?{
~A. Accept it automatically because all feedback must be used.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated against the writer's purpose.
~B. Reject all feedback forever.#Incorrect. One mismatched suggestion does not make all feedback useless.
~C. Post the rough draft without revising.#Incorrect. The writer still needs a publication workflow.
=D. Consider the suggestion, then choose a revision that supports the quiet reflective purpose.#Correct. This uses collaboration while protecting the intended effect.
}

::CW_U05_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which item best prepares an online reader before a creative piece?{
=A. A clear title and a brief author note when context is helpful.#Correct. These elements can help the reader enter the piece.
~B. A random symbol instead of a title.#Incorrect. Random symbols usually do not prepare the reader clearly.
~C. A long explanation of every line.#Incorrect. Overexplaining can replace the creative work. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. No title, no formatting, and no final check.#Incorrect. That weakens publication readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which checklist best fits online collaborative publication?{
~A. Type, post, ignore feedback, skip format.#Incorrect. This skips the publication process. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Change everything peers suggest, even if purpose disappears.#Incorrect. Collaboration should support purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Check purpose, consider feedback, confirm format, prepare the reader, and complete a final readiness check.#Correct. This matches the publication workflow. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Only choose a font and ignore meaning.#Incorrect. Publication quality is not just visual decoration.
}
